                    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
                    "body": "Now that I'm exploring this a bit more, it seems the fully qualified buffer names only occur when I use mini.starter's recent file to open the buffer, or when I'm using `Pick lsp` to navigate to code. \r\n\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/5642902/d12f4218-72c9-4ec9-b4a2-59a03269b38b\r\n\r\nIn the above screen cap I\r\n1. Use vim's edit command to open the most recent file, buffers shows relative file\r\n2. I use starter's recent file list to open the most recent file, fully qualified file winds up in buffer list\r\n3. I use `Pick lsp` via a keyboard shortcut, newly opened buffer has fully qualified file name \r\n4. I close vim and re-open and repeat 1 for demonstration purposes. \r\n\r\nDon't these plugins need to provide the relative path when opening the buffer to preserve this? My assumption is that the plugins are opening the buffers as fully qualified?",
                    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Now that I'm exploring this a bit more, it seems the fully qualified buffer names only occur when I use mini.starter's recent file to open the buffer, or when I'm using <code class=\"notranslate\">Pick lsp</code> to navigate to code.</p>\n<details open=\"\" class=\"details-reset border rounded-2\">\n  <summary class=\"tmp-px-3 py-2\">\n    <svg aria-hidden=\"true\" data-component=\"Octicon\" height=\"16\" viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" version=\"1.1\" width=\"16\" data-view-component=\"true\" class=\"octicon octicon-device-camera-video\">\n    <path d=\"M16 3.75v8.5a.75.75 0 0 1-1.136.643L11 10.575v.675A1.75 1.75 0 0 1 9.25 13h-7.5A1.75 1.75 0 0 1 0 11.25v-6.5C0 3.784.784 3 1.75 3h7.5c.966 0 1.75.784 1.75 1.75v.675l3.864-2.318A.75.75 0 0 1 16 3.75Zm-6.5 1a.25.25 0 0 0-.25-.25h-7.5a.25.25 0 0 0-.25.25v6.5c0 .138.112.25.25.25h7.5a.25.25 0 0 0 .25-.25v-6.5ZM11 8.825l3.5 2.1v-5.85l-3.5 2.1Z\"></path>\n</svg>\n    <span class=\"m-1\">2024-07-10T10.35.59-04.00.mp4</span>\n    <span class=\"dropdown-caret\"></span>\n  </summary>\n\n  <video src=\"https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/5642902/347448610-d12f4218-72c9-4ec9-b4a2-59a03269b38b.mp4?jwt=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..fzrWf_8DFXGHedMgk2BBg7F-0smQ_No1AQI60AJuTts\" data-canonical-src=\"https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/5642902/347448610-d12f4218-72c9-4ec9-b4a2-59a03269b38b.mp4?jwt=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..fzrWf_8DFXGHedMgk2BBg7F-0smQ_No1AQI60AJuTts\" controls=\"controls\" muted=\"muted\" class=\"d-block rounded-bottom-2 border-top width-fit\" style=\"max-height:640px; min-height: 200px\">\n\n  </video>\n</details>\n\n<p dir=\"auto\">In the above screen cap I</p>\n<ol dir=\"auto\">\n<li>Use vim's edit command to open the most recent file, buffers shows relative file</li>\n<li>I use starter's recent file list to open the most recent file, fully qualified file winds up in buffer list</li>\n<li>I use <code class=\"notranslate\">Pick lsp</code> via a keyboard shortcut, newly opened buffer has fully qualified file name</li>\n<li>I close vim and re-open and repeat 1 for demonstration purposes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Don't these plugins need to provide the relative path when opening the buffer to preserve this? My assumption is that the plugins are opening the buffers as fully qualified?</p>",
                    "bodyText": "Now that I'm exploring this a bit more, it seems the fully qualified buffer names only occur when I use mini.starter's recent file to open the buffer, or when I'm using Pick lsp to navigate to code.\n\n  \n    \n    \n\n    2024-07-10T10.35.59-04.00.mp4\n    \n  \n\n  \n\n  \n\n\nIn the above screen cap I\n\nUse vim's edit command to open the most recent file, buffers shows relative file\nI use starter's recent file list to open the most recent file, fully qualified file winds up in buffer list\nI use Pick lsp via a keyboard shortcut, newly opened buffer has fully qualified file name\nI close vim and re-open and repeat 1 for demonstration purposes.\n\nDon't these plugins need to provide the relative path when opening the buffer to preserve this? My assumption is that the plugins are opening the buffers as fully qualified?",
